  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U47", "U38", "U16", "U13", "J13", "J1"],
      "pins": ["U47.16", "U38.16", "U47.15", "U38.15", "U47.14", "U38.14", "U47.13", "U38.13", "U16.7", "U13.7", "U16.6", "U13.6"],
      "nets": ["NET0035", "NET0136", "NET0053", "NET0074", "NET0135", "NET0193"],
      "obs": "The two MCP2515 CAN controllers (U47 on the CAN BUS sheet, U38 on the CAN BUS continuation) are wired to identical global labels on every SPI line: SI=NET0053, SO/MISO=NET0136, CS=NET0035, SCK=NET0074 (also INT=NET0132, RESET=NET0164). Their isolated transceivers likewise share the physical bus pair CANH=NET0135 / CANL=NET0193, and both M12 connectors J13 (CAN Interface A) and J1 (CAN Interface B) carry the same NET0135/NET0193 on their CANH/CANL pins.",
      "expected": "The specification states the board implements 'two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9)'. Each controller must have its own chip-select and its own CANH/CANL pair so the two buses are electrically separate and independently addressable.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U47 pins S1=NET0053, S0=NET0136, CS=NET0035, SCK=NET0074 (CAN BUS sheet); U38 pins S1=NET0053, S0=NET0136, CS=NET0035, SCK=NET0074 (CAN BUS continuation)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U16 CANH=NET0135, CANL=NET0193; U13 CANH=NET0135, CANL=NET0193; J13 and J1 both on NET0135/NET0193"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9)"}
      ],
      "impact": "With CS tied together both MCP2515 devices are selected simultaneously and both drive the shared MISO (NET0136), causing bus contention; the two transceivers also drive the same CANH/CANL pair. The intended CAN0 and CAN1 are effectively merged into a single, non-functional bus, so independent communication with the two Odrive motor-controller branches is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U8", "U10"],
      "pins": ["U8.10", "U10.10", "U8.11", "U10.11", "U8.12", "U10.12", "U8.13", "U10.13"],
      "nets": ["NET0169", "NET0201", "NET0093", "NET0049"],
      "obs": "Both SC16IS752 UART/SPI expander chips (U8 and U10) are tied to the same global labels A0/CS=NET0169, A1/SI=NET0201, SD=NET0093, SCL/SCLK=NET0049, i.e. their chip-selects and MISO are shared.",
      "expected": "Two SPI peripherals on one bus require separate chip-selects (and only one device driving MISO at a time); sharing A0/CS and SD between U8 and U10 merges the two serial interfaces.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U8 pins A0/CS=NET0169, A1/SI=NET0201, SD=NET0093, SCL/SCLK=NET0049; U10 pins A0/CS=NET0169, A1/SI=NET0201, SD=NET0093, SCL/SCLK=NET0049 (Serial sheet)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The two UART expanders contend on the SPI MISO line and are both selected together, so the serial ports they provide cannot be addressed independently.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing appears to merge what the specification describes as two independent CAN buses (and, by the same global-label pattern, two SPI UART expanders): the two MCP2515 controllers and their transceivers share identical CS/MISO/SI/SCK and CANH/CANL nets, so the buses are not separate and the SPI lines contend. Reference designators differ from the spec's netlist (a build/revision difference, not flagged), but the shared-label bridging of the CAN0/CAN1 control and bus nets is a deviation from the intended two-bus architecture."
}

